# Theme elements: theme

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`theme()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/theme.html), in the order the page gives them.
Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps
its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

A theme draws everything in a plot that is not data: titles, labels, fonts, backgrounds, gridlines,
legends and strips. `theme()` changes parts of one plot's theme. Each part is an element, such as
`element_text()`, `element_line()`, `element_rect()`, or `element_blank()` to draw nothing, and an
element takes what it does not set from a more general one: `axis.title.x.bottom` from
`axis.title.x`, that from `axis.title`, and that from `text`. Every Giggly plot is drawn in
ggplot2's default theme, `theme_gray()`, and there is no way to change it yet. A plot is described
with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell
aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and
shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data in these examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`,
R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* US magazine, which the setup cell binds to
`mtcars`, R's name for it.

## A plot with a title

```r
p1 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  labs(title = "Fuel economy declines as weight increases")
p1
```

```elixir
p1 =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
  |> Plot.geom_point()
  |> Plot.labs(title: "Fuel economy declines as weight increases")

render.(p1)
```

## The plot's title and background

```r
# Plot ---------------------------------------------------------------------
p1 + theme(plot.title = element_text(size = rel(2)))
p1 + theme(plot.background = element_rect(fill = "green"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the title's size and the background behind the whole
plot.

## The panel's background, border and gridlines

```r
# Panels --------------------------------------------------------------------

p1 + theme(panel.background = element_rect(fill = "white", colour = "grey50"))
p1 + theme(panel.border = element_rect(linetype = "dashed"))
p1 + theme(panel.grid.major = element_line(colour = "black"))
p1 + theme(
  panel.grid.major.y = element_blank(),
  panel.grid.minor.y = element_blank()
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the panel's background, its border and its gridlines.

## Gridlines over the data

```r
# Put gridlines on top of data
p1 + theme(
  panel.background = element_rect(fill = NA),
  panel.grid.major = element_line(colour = "grey50"),
  panel.ontop = TRUE
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here drawing the panel's gridlines over the points.

## Axis lines, text and ticks

```r
# Axes ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Change styles of axes texts and lines
p1 + theme(axis.line = element_line(linewidth = 3, colour = "grey80"))
p1 + theme(axis.text = element_text(colour = "blue"))
p1 + theme(axis.ticks = element_line(linewidth = 2))
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the axes' lines, text and ticks.

## The y axis's title

```r
# Change the appearance of the y-axis title
p1 + theme(axis.title.y = element_text(size = rel(1.5), angle = 90))
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the size and angle of the y axis's title.

## The length and direction of ticks

```r
# Make ticks point outwards on y-axis and inwards on x-axis
p1 + theme(
  axis.ticks.length.y = unit(.25, "cm"),
  axis.ticks.length.x = unit(-.25, "cm"),
  axis.text.x = element_text(margin = margin(t = .3, unit = "cm"))
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the length of the ticks and the margin above the x
axis's text.

## A plot with legends

```r
# Legend --------------------------------------------------------------------
p2 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = factor(cyl), shape = factor(vs))) +
  labs(
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Fuel economy (mpg)",
    colour = "Cylinders",
    shape = "Transmission"
   )
p2
```

Giggly writes `factor(cyl)` as `{:factor, [:cyl]}`.

```elixir
p2 =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
  |> Plot.geom_point(%{colour: {:factor, [:cyl]}, shape: {:factor, [:vs]}})
  |> Plot.labs(
    x: "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y: "Fuel economy (mpg)",
    colour: "Cylinders",
    shape: "Transmission"
  )

render.(p2)
```

## Where the legends go

```r
# Position
p2 + theme(legend.position = "none")
p2 + theme(legend.justification = "top")
p2 + theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here where the legends go.

## Legends inside the panel

```r
# Or place legends inside the plot using relative coordinates between 0 and 1
# legend.justification sets the corner that the position refers to
p2 + theme(
  legend.position = "inside",
  legend.position.inside = c(.95, .95),
  legend.justification = c("right", "top"),
  legend.box.just = "right",
  legend.margin = margin_auto(6)
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here placing the legends inside the panel.

## A box around the legends

```r
# The legend.box properties work similarly for the space around
# all the legends
p2 + theme(
  legend.box.background = element_rect(),
  legend.box.margin = margin_auto(6)
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the box around the legends and its margin.

## Legend keys, text and titles

```r
# You can also control the display of the keys
# and the justification related to the plot area can be set
p2 + theme(legend.key = element_rect(fill = "white", colour = "black"))
p2 + theme(legend.text = element_text(size = 8, colour = "red"))
p2 + theme(legend.title = element_text(face = "bold"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the legends' keys, text and titles.

## Strips

ggplot2's `~ cyl` names the column to facet by; Giggly names it as a mapping does, by an atom or
a string.

```r
# Strips --------------------------------------------------------------------

p3 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(~ cyl)
p3
```

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.facet_wrap(:cyl)
|> render.()
```

```r
p3 + theme(strip.background = element_rect(colour = "black", fill = "white"))
p3 + theme(strip.text.x = element_text(colour = "white", face = "bold"))
# More direct strip.text.x here for top
# as in the facet_wrap the default strip.position is "top"
p3 + theme(strip.text.x.top = element_text(colour = "white", face = "bold"))
p3 + theme(panel.spacing = unit(1, "lines"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the strips' background and text and the space
between the panels.

## Default colours for layers

```r
# Colours -------------------------------------------------------------------

p4 <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  annotate(label = "Text Annotation", x = 5, y = 30, geom = "text")

# You can use the 'ink' setting to set colour defaults for all layers
p4 + theme(geom = element_geom(ink = "dodgerblue"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: changing the theme, here the colour every layer draws in.

## Accent and paper colours

```r
# Alternate colours are derived from the 'accent' and 'paper' settings
p4 + geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x) +
  theme(geom = element_geom(accent = "tomato", paper = "orchid"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()` with a line fitted by `lm()`, and changing the theme, here the
accent and paper colours layers draw with.

## A default palette

```r
# You can also set default palettes in the theme
p4 + aes(colour = drat) +
  theme(palette.colour.continuous = c("white", "pink", "hotpink"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: adding a mapping with `+ aes()`, and changing the theme, here the palette a
continuous `colour` scale draws from.